          "deepDive": "**〜んばかり** comes from the classical negative ぬ (→ ん) + ばかり: *all but ~ / on the verge of ~*. Nothing actually happens — the verb describes what something **looks as if it is about to do**, which makes the description vivid and exaggerated: 折れんばかりに揺れる (swaying so hard they look about to snap), 泣き出さんばかりの顔 (a face on the verge of tears).\n\nConnection: ない-stem + んばかり: 言わ・泣き出さ・あふれ・壊さ + んばかり. **する → せんばかり** (墜落せんばかり). It works like an adverb/noun modifier: **〜んばかりに + verb**, **〜んばかりの + noun**, or predicate **〜んばかりだ**.\n\nTypical collocations: 言わんばかりに / 言わんばかりの顔・態度 (the most common), 泣かんばかり, 飛び上がらんばかりに喜ぶ, あふれんばかりの笑顔・情熱, 溢れんばかりの人. The subject is often a person's expression or attitude, or a physical thing seen in motion.\n\nThe note: **〜とばかりに** omits 言わん — 待ってましたとばかりに, 今だとばかりに, このときとばかりに (*seizing the moment*). It quotes the attitude someone seems to have.\n\nCompare:\n- **〜そうだ / 〜そうな** (N4): neutral *looks like it's about to*. んばかり is literary, more dramatic, and implies an extreme degree.\n- **〜かのように** (N2): *as if* (comparison to something unreal). んばかり is narrower: the action is imminent, not just similar.\n- **〜ばかりだ / 〜ばかりに** (N2 *keep ~ing / just because*): different ばかり — check for the ん before it.\n\nPitfall: ✗言うばかりに. The form must be the ない-stem + ん. Written/literary style; in casual speech people would say 今にも〜しそう.\n\nJLPT tip: an option ending in 〜ん (言わん, 泣かん, 飛び上がらん) before ばかり is a strong signal; also watch for 今にも in the sentence.",
