          "deepDive": "**〜たら／〜ば それまでだ** says *if ~ happens, that's the end of it — everything so far is wasted / nothing more can be done*. それまで = *up to that point*: the condition marks the point where everything stops.\n\nConnection: V-たら or V-ば + それまでだ; the negative 〜なければそれまでだ is common (使いこなせる技術者がいなければそれまでだ). Stylistically neutral to slightly written; also said as a warning in speech (部長: 先を越されたらそれまでだぞ).\n\nTypical use:\n- A warning: all your preparation is for nothing if X happens (病気になったらそれまでだ).\n- A resigned observation about fragility: 人間死んでしまえばそれまでだ.\n- Encouragement framed negatively: あきらめたらそれまでだ (*if you give up, it's over — so don't*).\n\nDon't confuse with **〜までだ / 〜までのことだ** (#82, *(if so) I'll simply do ~*): 駄目ならやめるまでだ. #82 attaches to the dictionary form and announces the speaker's fallback action; #74 attaches to a conditional and says everything ends. Also different from **〜たまでだ** (#93, *I merely did ~*).\n\nCompare **〜ば／〜たら終わりだ**: nearly the same meaning, more colloquial. それまでだ sounds a little more philosophical.\n\nJLPT tip: in paraphrase questions, それまでだ ≈ 〜たら使えない／出られない／何にもならない (the practice answers: バッテリーが切れたら使えない, 2回戦には出られない).",
