          "deepDive": "**V-るまでのことだ / V-るまでだ** = *(if so,) I'll simply ~; there's nothing for it but ~*. Usually preceded by a condition (〜たら, 〜なら, 〜なければ): the speaker states a fallback action and presents it as no big deal. まで = *that far and no further* — the only thing left to do.\n\n- Mostly about the speaker's own actions (自分について使うことが多い).\n- Sounds calm and resolute; as the book notes, it can mask real concern (部長: 採算が取れなければあきらめるまでのことだが……).\n- までだ is shorter and a bit blunter; までのことだ is slightly more measured.\n\nContrast carefully (a favorite test point):\n- **V-たまでだ / V-たまでのことだ** (#93, see the ☞ line): *I merely did ~ (nothing special)* — past form, used to downplay one's action or justify it: 当たり前のことをしたまでのことである.\n- **〜たら／〜ばそれまでだ** (#74): *if ~, it's all over*. That's about everything being wasted, not about a fallback plan.\n- **〜しかない / 〜ほかない** (N2): *have no choice but ~* — heavier, more reluctant. までだ sounds more carefree.\n- **〜までもない** (#41): *there's no need to ~*.\n\nPitfall: dictionary form for the fallback (✗行ったまでのことだ in this meaning).\n\nJLPT tip: paraphrase = 〜すればいい (the practice answer: 裁判に訴えればいい; 買えばいいから困らない).",
